text:
Summarize repeated recovery
For simulation s, let \widehat\theta_s be the estimate and \theta_s the generating value. Across S simulations, estimated bias is

\widehat{\operatorname{Bias}}
=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}(\widehat\theta_s-\theta_s),

and root mean squared error is

\operatorname{RMSE}
=\sqrt{\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}
(\widehat\theta_s-\theta_s)^2}.

If the procedure returns intervals, a simulation study estimates its coverage probability with the proportion of simulated intervals containing the generating value. For a confidence interval, coverage probability is a repeated-sampling property of the interval procedure. Bayesian posterior intervals can also be checked for simulation-based calibration, but their interpretation for one fitted dataset remains posterior rather than repeated sampling. The code below uses the exact binomial confidence interval introduced earlier. Bias, root mean squared error, and coverage probability answer different questions. Small average bias can coexist with large errors that cancel across simulations.

Turn the product into rank one terms
Let \mathbf{u}_k be column k of \mathbf{U} and \mathbf{v}_k be column k of \mathbf{V}. Matrix multiplication lets us rewrite the SVD as

\mathbf{X}
=
\sum_{k=1}^{r}
d_k\mathbf{u}_k\mathbf{v}_k^{\mathsf T}.

Each term

d_k\mathbf{u}_k\mathbf{v}_k^{\mathsf T}

is rank one. The left vector gives the relative expression of pattern k across rows. The right vector gives the relative participation of the columns. The singular value gives the pattern’s overall magnitude.
Unlike an arbitrary collection of outer products, the terms in the singular value decomposition are orthogonal and sorted by singular value.

Define the array and its indices
Let there be n linguistic objects and p measured variables. A data matrix is

\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times p}.

Its entries are

x_{ij}
=
\text{the value of variable }j\text{ for object }i.

The complete row for object i is

\mathbf{x}_i^{\mathsf T}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
x_{i1} & x_{i2} & \cdots & x_{ip}
\end{bmatrix}.

The complete column for variable j is

\mathbf{x}_{\cdot j}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
x_{1j} & x_{2j} & \cdots & x_{nj}
\end{bmatrix}^{\mathsf T}.

These two views answer different questions. A row asks how one object is represented across variables. A column asks how one variable changes across objects.

Scaling changes the unit
The sample standard deviation of column j is

s_j
=
\sqrt{
\frac{1}{n-1}
\sum_{i=1}^{n}
(x_{ij}-\bar{x}_j)^2
}.

For the duration values, the sum of squared centered values is

(-20)^2+0^2+20^2=800.

Thus, the sample variance is 800/(3-1)=400, and the sample standard deviation is 20 ms. Dividing the centered values by 20 gives

\begin{bmatrix}
-20/20\\0/20\\20/20
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
-1\\0\\1
\end{bmatrix}.

The standardized entry is generally

z_{ij}
=
\frac{x_{ij}-\bar{x}_j}{s_j}.

The transformed column has sample mean zero and sample standard deviation one. A difference of one unit now means a difference of one observed standard deviation for that variable.

Keep preprocessing inside the training fold
Suppose the raw features require standardization. For each validation split, estimate

\bar{x}^{\mathrm{train}}_m
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
s^{\mathrm{train}}_m

from the training rows only. Transform both sets using those training values:

z^{\mathrm{train}}_{im}
=
\frac{x^{\mathrm{train}}_{im}-\bar{x}^{\mathrm{train}}_m}
{s^{\mathrm{train}}_m}

and

z^{\mathrm{test}}_{im}
=
\frac{x^{\mathrm{test}}_{im}-\bar{x}^{\mathrm{train}}_m}
{s^{\mathrm{train}}_m}.

Then fit centroids to the transformed training rows and score transformed test rows against those fixed centroids.
This sequence prevents data leakage. Test rows cannot influence the origin, scale, or centers used to evaluate them.

Define the represented objects
Let N linguistic objects be measured on p numerical properties. Their representation is

\mathbf{X}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
x_{11}&\cdots&x_{1p}\\
\vdots&\ddots&\vdots\\
x_{N1}&\cdots&x_{Np}
\end{bmatrix}
\in
\mathbb{R}^{N\times p}.

Row i is the vector

\mathbf{x}_i
=
\begin{bmatrix}
x_{i1}&\cdots&x_{ip}
\end{bmatrix},

which contains every property allowed to make object i similar to another object. A learned cluster assignment attaches to this row unit.
If one row is a participant, a cluster describes participants with similar recorded profiles. If survey responses are averaged within households before the matrix is built, one row becomes a household and the learned groups describe households. The algorithm may be identical, but the unit of interpretation has changed.

Define one Gaussian component
Let \mathbf{x}_i\in\mathbb{R}^p be the measurements for token i. Component k has mean vector

\boldsymbol{\mu}_k
\in
\mathbb{R}^p

and covariance matrix

\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k
\in
\mathbb{R}^{p\times p}.

Its density is

\mathcal{N}
(\mathbf{x}_i;
\boldsymbol{\mu}_k,
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k)
=
\frac{
\exp\left[
-\frac{1}{2}
(\mathbf{x}_i-\boldsymbol{\mu}_k)^{\mathsf T}
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k^{-1}
(\mathbf{x}_i-\boldsymbol{\mu}_k)
\right]
}{
(2\pi)^{p/2}
|\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k|^{1/2}
}.

The mean locates the component center. The covariance controls its scale and orientation. In two formant dimensions, equal-density contours form ellipses when the covariance is unrestricted.

Define Euclidean distance
For two rows \mathbf{x}_i and \mathbf{x}_j with p features, Euclidean distance is

d(\mathbf{x}_i,\mathbf{x}_j)
=
\sqrt{
\sum_{m=1}^{p}
(x_{im}-x_{jm})^2
}.

The calculation has three steps. Subtract the values within each column, square the differences, and take the square root of their sum.
Squaring makes positive and negative differences contribute in the same direction. The final square root returns the distance to the units of the coordinate system, though a system with several differently measured columns has no single physical unit.

Estimate scaling within training data
During held-out evaluation, column means and standard deviations are fitted quantities. Estimate them from the training rows:

\bar{x}^{\mathrm{train}}_m
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
s^{\mathrm{train}}_m.

Apply those values to a test row:

z^{\mathrm{test}}_{im}
=
\frac{
x^{\mathrm{test}}_{im}-\bar{x}^{\mathrm{train}}_m
}{
s^{\mathrm{train}}_m
}.

Computing scaling from the complete dataset would allow test rows to influence the geometry used to evaluate them. Computing separate test means would put training and test rows in different coordinate systems.
Estimating the scaling transformation from held-out rows is data leakage. Out-of-sample evaluation requires one coordinate system estimated without those rows.

Decide what one means
Now suppose each participant contributes 100 trials. Row-level LOO removes one trial but leaves 99 trials from the same participant in training. It also tends to leave other responses to the same item in training. This may be appropriate for predicting another trial from observed participants and items.
It does not evaluate prediction for an unadapted participant absent during fitting. For that question, one participant and all of that participant’s rows must be omitted together. If there are S participants, the procedure makes S fits:

\widehat{R}_{\text{leave-subject-out}}
=
\frac{1}{S}
\sum_{s=1}^{S}
\frac{1}{n_s}
\sum_{i:g(i)=s}
L(y_i,\widehat{f}_{-s}(x_i)),

where \widehat{f}_{-s} is fit without participant s. This is often called leave-one-subject-out validation. The new idea is not a different loss or fitting method. It is a different meaning of one.
The same reasoning applies to items and texts. Prediction for an omitted passage requires omitting a complete passage, not one position in that passage. Prediction for an omitted verb requires omitting every token headed by one verb. The next page develops this grouping decision directly.

The algorithm
Partition the observation indices into disjoint folds F_1,\ldots,F_K. For fold k:

fit the entire analysis procedure to observations outside F_k;
use that fitted procedure to predict observations in F_k; and
store the predictions in their original rows.

After all folds have been processed, the cross-validated risk estimate is

\widehat{R}_{\mathrm{CV}}
=
\frac{1}{N}
\sum_{k=1}^K
\sum_{i\in F_k}
L\left(y_i,\widehat{f}_{-k}(x_i)\right),

where \widehat{f}_{-k} is fit without fold k. The model changes across folds, but the procedure used to fit it remains fixed.

Write one joint distribution
For listener j, collect the intercept and slope deviations in a vector:

\begin{bmatrix}
b_{0j}\\
b_{1j}
\end{bmatrix}.

Model the vector with

\begin{bmatrix}
b_{0j}\\b_{1j}
\end{bmatrix}
\sim
\mathcal{N}
\left(
\begin{bmatrix}0\\0\end{bmatrix},
\begin{bmatrix}
\tau_0^2 & \rho\tau_0\tau_1\\
\rho\tau_0\tau_1 & \tau_1^2
\end{bmatrix}
\right).

The parameter \rho is the correlation between listener intercept deviations and listener slope deviations. The covariance is \rho\tau_0\tau_1.
The correlation \rho asks whether groups above or below the population intercept also tend to have slopes above or below the population slope.

Interpret a posterior contrast
Suppose we want the conditional-mean difference between 3 and 7 bits. In centered units these values are -2 and 2. For every posterior draw,

\begin{aligned}
\Delta^{(s)}
&=m^{(s)}(2)-m^{(s)}(-2)\\
&=4\beta_1^{(s)}.
\end{aligned}

The posterior distribution of 4\beta_1 directly represents uncertainty about the four-bit contrast. This derived quantity preserves joint posterior uncertainty because it is calculated within each draw.

Choose one discrepancy
A discrepancy function T(y) reduces a complete dataset to a feature that the model might fail to reproduce. Linguistic examples include:

the proportion of slider responses at an endpoint;
the standard deviation of reaction times within a frequency band;
the number of zero construction counts per document;
a difference between two condition means;
the proportion of no, maybe, and yes responses within an embedding environment.

The check compares the observed value

T(y)

with the replicated values

T(y^{\mathrm{rep},(1)}),\ldots,
T(y^{\mathrm{rep},(S)}).

The same function must be applied to observed and replicated data. If the observed statistic excludes practice trials or averages within speakers, every replicated statistic must make the same choices.

Check complete and targeted features
An overlay of replicated distributions can reveal failures in center, spread, skew, or tails:
bayesplot::ppc_dens_overlay(
  y = dat$rt,
  yrep = y_rep[1:100, ]
)
The overlay is a broad check. It may show that the observed right tail extends beyond nearly every replicate, but it can also become visually crowded. A targeted discrepancy connects the failure more directly to a claim.
For a bounded response model, a useful sequence is:

compare lower endpoint mass;
compare upper endpoint mass;
compare the interior distribution;
repeat the three checks within the focal linguistic conditions.

An overall density can recover aggregate endpoint mass while missing that one condition produces nearly all upper endpoints. The conditional check detects this cancellation.

Specify the population unit
A population must be a population of something. It might contain

speakers in a speech community;
utterances produced in a genre;
lexical types represented by a dictionary;
sentence items licensed by a construction;
judgments from a class of comprehenders.

These units are not interchangeable. Ten thousand tokens from five speakers provide many utterance observations but only five sampled speakers.
If the target concerns speakers, the speaker count supplies the direct replication at that level. If the target concerns tokens from those five speakers during the recorded sessions, the larger token count may be relevant. The unit specified in the population claim determines which count matters.

Standardize the discrepancy
Let \widehat{\mu}_D be the sample mean difference. Its realized estimate is \widehat{\mu}_d. Let \widehat{\operatorname{SE}}(\widehat{\mu}_D) be its estimated standard error. The statistic

Z
=
\frac{
\widehat{\mu}_D-\mu_{D,0}
}{
\widehat{\operatorname{SE}}(\widehat{\mu}_D)
}

expresses the discrepancy from the null value \mu_{D,0} in standard-error units.
Suppose the estimate is 12 ms, the null value is 0 ms, and the estimated standard error is 5 ms. Then

z_{\mathrm{obs}}
=\frac{12-0}{5}
=2.4.

The numerator records direction and magnitude in milliseconds. The denominator records sampling uncertainty in milliseconds. Their ratio is unitless. A statistic of 2.4 says that the estimate is 2.4 estimated standard errors above the null value.
The statistic is not a probability. To calculate a probability, we need its null distribution, which is the distribution of statistic values generated when the null hypothesis and the sampling assumptions hold.

Quantify Monte Carlo error
The bootstrap page first distinguished simulation error from sampling uncertainty. In the present setting, variation caused by using a finite number S of simulation draws is Monte Carlo error. For independent draws and a binary indicator, an estimated Monte Carlo standard error is

\widehat{\operatorname{MCSE}}(\widehat{I}_S)
=
\sqrt{
\frac{
\widehat{I}_S(1-\widehat{I}_S)
}{S}
}.



Code
mcse &lt;- sqrt(mc_estimate * (1 - mc_estimate) / draw_count)
stopifnot(mcse &lt; .003)
c(estimate = mc_estimate, MCSE = mcse)


Multiplying S by four tends to divide MCSE by two. This square-root rate is the same rate that appears in sampling standard errors, but the source of variation differs.
The law of large numbers supplies the basic justification. As S grows, the average of independent target draws tends to approach the target expectation when that expectation exists. A central limit theorem motivates an approximate normal distribution for many Monte Carlo averages and the MCSE calculation above.
This result does not guarantee that every finite simulation is precise enough. Monte Carlo error should be small relative to posterior uncertainty and the precision used in substantive claims. Reporting six decimal places when MCSE is .003 gives false numerical precision.

Distinguish three operations
Suppose a normal model has condition means mu_a and mu_b and response standard deviation sigma. A generated-quantities block can contain
generated quantities {
  real mean_difference;
  array[N] real y_rep;
  vector[N] log_lik;

  mean_difference = mu_a - mu_b;

  for (n in 1:N) {
    real mu_n = condition[n] == 1 ? mu_a : mu_b;
    y_rep[n] = normal_rng(mu_n, sigma);
    log_lik[n] = normal_lpdf(y[n] | mu_n, sigma);
  }
}
The first line computes a deterministic transformation of the joint parameter draw. The second operation simulates a future response. The third evaluates the log density of an observed response.

See the problem with a normal interval
The Wald interval inserts the observed proportion into a normal approximation and estimated standard error formula:

\widehat{\pi}
\mathbin{\pm}
1.96\sqrt{
\frac{\widehat{\pi}(1-\widehat{\pi})}{n}
}.

For 18 successes in 20 trials, the estimated standard error is

\sqrt{\frac{.90(.10)}{20}}
\approx.0671.

The resulting interval is

.90\mathbin{\pm}1.96(.0671)
\approx[.7685,1.0315].

The upper endpoint exceeds one, even though a probability cannot. At 20 successes in 20 trials, the estimated standard error would be zero and the interval would collapse to a point. These failures arise because the normal approximation does not represent the finite and bounded binomial sampling distribution well near a boundary.

Task 7: license the claim (5 points)
Write a six-to-eight sentence results paragraph that does all of the following:

names the ordered response and the two-model sequence;
reports the factivity direction and uncertainty;
reports variation among annotators, discourses, and predicates;
reports the embedding model comparison with its predictive target;
describes the category-level factivity and negation pattern;
reports the modal-filter sensitivity; and
limits the claim to these naturally occurring corpus discourses and the recorded annotators.

Do not say that factivity determines projection. The intended conclusion is narrower: factivity supplies a positive but coarse shift, while embedding environment and the modeled grouping units account for additional structure in commitment judgments.

Data, source, and target
The original data come from the Provo Corpus, with the theoretical analysis in Luke and Christianson (2016). The corpus and its files are available from OSF under CC BY 4.0.
data/provo-content-word-positions.csv is an adapted teaching table. One row represents one content-word position. The response mean_log_ffd is the mean log first-fixation duration over observed readers who fixated that position. Thus the analysis is conditional on fixation and targets an observed-reader mean, not individual reading behavior.


Code
items &lt;- read.csv("data/provo-content-word-positions.csv")
dim(items)
head(items)


The principal columns are:

position_id: repaired passage by position identifier;
Text_ID: passage identifier;
Word_POS: adjective, adverb, noun, or verb;
Word_Length and Word_In_Sentence_Number: control predictors;
OrthoMatchModel: smoothed orthographic cloze probability;
POSMatchModel: smoothed part-of-speech match probability;
LSA_Response_Match_Score: mean semantic similarity between cloze responses and the target; and
mean_log_ffd: the response.

The published Word_Unique_ID is not a valid key by itself: QID2687 occurs in Texts 18 and 55. The preparation script constructs position_id from passage and word position.

Task 1: state the directional theory (15 points)
Create


Code
items$pp &lt;- as.integer(items$recipient_realization == "PP")
items$relative_log_length &lt;-
  log(items$theme_length_words) - log(items$recipient_length_words)


Then state the predicted coefficient direction for each of the following contrasts. In every case, connect the sign to the argument order in a PP. Do not write only that an early argument should precede a late one.

relative log length;
a given rather than accessible recipient;
a new rather than accessible recipient;
a pronominal rather than nonpronominal recipient;
a given rather than accessible theme;
a new rather than accessible theme;
a pronominal rather than nonpronominal theme; and
an inanimate rather than animate recipient.

Note that a positive value of relative_log_length means that the theme is longer than the recipient. A PP would place that longer theme first. Thus the sign of this coefficient must be reasoned through from the variable definition and the response coding.

Task 5: license the claim (10 points)
Write a five-to-seven sentence results paragraph that does all of the following:

names the response and model;
summarizes the directional coefficient pattern;
reports the two profile probabilities;
reports the held-out-verb Brier comparison;
identifies the tell limitation; and
states why these corpus tokens do not license a claim about every English speaker, genre, or dative context.

Do not say that harmonic alignment “determines” dative choice. The model estimates graded shifts in a corpus probability.

Implement the rule in base R


Code
draw_boundary &lt;- function(n) {
  u &lt;- runif(n)
  label &lt;- c("weak", "intermediate", "strong")
  endpoint &lt;- c(.50, .80, 1)

  label[vapply(u, function(value) {
    which(value &lt;= endpoint)[1]
  }, integer(1))]
}

draw_boundary(10)


The code follows the definition literally: draw U, find the first cumulative endpoint at or above it, and return the corresponding label.
With many draws, the empirical category proportions should approach .50, .30, and $.20. A large discrepancy may expose an interval or ordering error.

Compare parameter values without Z
For two values \theta_a and \theta_b,

\frac{p(\theta_b\mid\mathbf{y})}
{p(\theta_a\mid\mathbf{y})}
=
\frac{\widetilde{p}(\theta_b\mid\mathbf{y})}
{\widetilde{p}(\theta_a\mid\mathbf{y})}.

The unknown constant cancels. Algorithms can compare relative posterior density even when they cannot directly normalize the complete curve.


Code
log_unnormalized &lt;- function(theta) {
  -2 * (theta - .50)^2 + dt(theta, df = 3, log = TRUE)
}

relative_density &lt;- exp(
  log_unnormalized(.50) - log_unnormalized(0)
)
normalizer &lt;- integrate(
  function(theta) exp(log_unnormalized(theta)),
  lower = -Inf,
  upper = Inf
)$value

stopifnot(relative_density &gt; 1)
stopifnot(normalizer &gt; 0)
c(relative_density_at_half_vs_zero = relative_density,
  normalizer = normalizer)


Numerical integration works here because the target is one dimensional. In a model with many parameters, direct quadrature may require an impractical number of evaluations.

Use the gradient to choose a direction
A Hamiltonian trajectory obeys two differential equations:

\frac{\mathrm{d}q}{\mathrm{d}t}
=
\frac{\partial H}{\partial p}
=p

and

\frac{\mathrm{d}p}{\mathrm{d}t}
=
-\frac{\partial H}{\partial q}
=-\nabla U(q).

The first equation uses the momentum to change the parameter vector. The second uses the gradient of the potential energy to change the momentum. Since U(q) is the negative log posterior, this gradient supplies local information about how posterior density changes around the current parameter value.
The resulting path differs from a random walk in a specific way. A random walk chooses a direction without consulting the target density. An HMC path repeatedly revises its direction using the gradient. This revision may keep a long path near a common energy level even when the posterior parameters are correlated.

Add independent sampling variances
For independent groups,

\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat{\Delta})
&=\operatorname{Var}(\overline{Y}_A-\overline{Y}_B)\\
&=\operatorname{Var}(\overline{Y}_A)
+\operatorname{Var}(\overline{Y}_B).
\end{aligned}

The minus sign disappears because multiplying a random variable by -1 does not change its variance. Independence makes the covariance term zero.
Estimate each population variance from its group:

\widehat{\operatorname{SE}}(\widehat{\Delta})
=
\sqrt{
\frac{s_A^2}{n_A}
+
\frac{s_B^2}{n_B}
}.

This is the standard error in Welch’s two-sample t-test. The two variance contributions can differ. The test does not assume that the population variances are equal.

Find the maximum
Differentiating and setting the derivative to zero gives

\widehat{\pi}_{\mathrm{MLE}}
=\frac{k}{n}.

For this extract,

\widehat{\pi}_{\mathrm{MLE}}
=\frac{3327}{12280}
\approx.271.

The maximum-likelihood estimate is the parameter value that maximizes the likelihood:

\widehat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}}
=\operatorname*{arg\,max}_{\theta}L(\theta;\mathbf{x}).

The notation arg max returns an argument, not a height. Here the argument is a candidate value of \pi. The maximum likelihood is the value of the function at that argument. In ordinary discussion, these two objects are sometimes conflated, but the estimate is the parameter value.

See why the normalizer cancels
Suppose p(\delta)=\widetilde{p}(\delta)/Z. The target part of the ratio is

\frac{\widetilde{p}(\delta')/Z}
{\widetilde{p}(\delta^{(s)})/Z}
=
\frac{\widetilde{p}(\delta')}
{\widetilde{p}(\delta^{(s)})}.

Metropolis-Hastings thus needs relative target density rather than a known normalizing constant.

Declare the observed data
data {
  int&lt;lower=0&gt; n;
  array[n] int&lt;lower=0, upper=1&gt; y;
}
The integer n is the number of clauses. The array y contains n observed integers constrained to zero or one. Values in the data block are supplied before sampling and remain fixed during posterior computation.
The constraints check allowable data values. If y contains a two, Stan rejects the input rather than silently treating it as a Bernoulli outcome.

Declare the unknown parameter
parameters {
  real&lt;lower=0, upper=1&gt; pi;
}
The parameter pi is unknown and must lie between zero and one. Stan internally transforms constrained parameters to an unconstrained scale for sampling and adjusts the density for that transformation.
The support constraint is not the prior. It says which values are allowable. The beta statement in the model block says how prior density varies across those values.

Add density contributions
model {
  pi ~ beta(2, 2);
  y ~ bernoulli(pi);
}
The first statement adds the beta prior log density for pi. The second adds one Bernoulli log likelihood contribution for every element of y.
Stan’s tilde notation is density notation in the model block. It does not simulate pi and then simulate y. The program is equivalent, up to constants not involving pi, to
model {
  target += beta_lpdf(pi | 2, 2);
  target += bernoulli_lpmf(y | pi);
}
The variable target accumulates the unnormalized log posterior density.

Revise the population model deliberately
Possible responses include:

removing an unsupported correlation while retaining both variances;
removing a slope variance that the design cannot identify;
collecting more levels or within group contrasts;
using a regularized model that retains uncertainty near the boundary.

These responses make different assumptions. Removing the correlation sets the intercept-slope correlation to zero. Removing a slope sets its between-group variance to zero. More data preserve the richer target but change the design.
Do not simplify solely to obtain a smaller standard error or a preferred significance result. The random effects structure encodes the populations across which the comparison should generalize.

Report the resolution
A singular-fit report should answer four questions:

the original random effects specification;
the diagnostic showing singularity;
the revised specification and its design justification;
whether the population comparison changes materially.

The singular fit is evidence about the relation between the requested covariance structure and the available data. Hiding the warning loses that evidence. Treating every singular fit as proof that group variation is exactly zero overstates it.






NoteCheck the dimensions



A model converges but estimates its random slope standard deviation as zero. Which diagnostic has failed? What design property would you inspect before removing the slope?


The next page combines group variation with nonnormal response models.

The usual inward nudge
A common workaround replaces endpoints with nearby interior values. For a small constant \delta, define

\widetilde Y_i=
\begin{cases}
\delta & \text{if }Y_i=0,\\
1-\delta & \text{if }Y_i=1,\\
Y_i & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}

If \delta=.001, for instance, an observed no becomes .001 and an observed yes becomes .999. The transformed values can be passed to ordinary beta software.
But the nudge does not extend the beta model to the endpoints. It changes the observations so that they satisfy the model. It also creates a researcher choice: why .001 rather than .01, .0001, or a value determined by the sample size? Calling the change “tiny” describes the response scale, not its effect on the likelihood.

An interval for group-level improvement
When held-out groups are treated as independent draws under a sampling model, a t interval for their mean loss difference is

\overline{\Delta}
\;\pm\;
t_{1-\alpha/2,G-1}
\frac{s_\Delta}{\sqrt{G}}.

The calculation is the same paired-mean procedure introduced earlier, now applied to predictive losses rather than acoustic measurements. Its assumptions concern the group differences. With few or highly heterogeneous groups, plotting every difference is more informative than relying on the interval alone.
A group bootstrap can resample the G held-out groups as an alternative approximation. Resampling individual positions would overstate the number of independent passages.

The separation rule
Let D_{\mathrm{train}} and D_{\mathrm{test}} be disjoint collections of observations. A fitted predictor is

\widehat{f}_{\mathrm{train}}
=
\operatorname{Fit}(D_{\mathrm{train}}),

and test loss is

\widehat{R}_{\mathrm{test}}
=
\frac{1}{|D_{\mathrm{test}}|}
\sum_{i\in D_{\mathrm{test}}}
L\left(y_i,\widehat{f}_{\mathrm{train}}(x_i)\right).

The subscript on \widehat{f} is essential. The test responses y_i must not influence the coefficients, feature transformations, model choice, or any other fitted quantity used to produce their predictions.

The expectation step uses responsibilities
Given \boldsymbol{\theta}^{(t)}, compute

r_{ik}^{(t)}
=
P(Z_i=k\mid\mathbf{x}_i,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{(t)}).

Using the formula from the previous page,

r_{ik}^{(t)}
=
\frac{
\pi_k^{(t)}
\mathcal{N}
(\mathbf{x}_i;
\boldsymbol{\mu}_k^{(t)},
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k^{(t)})
}{
\sum_{j=1}^{K}
\pi_j^{(t)}
\mathcal{N}
(\mathbf{x}_i;
\boldsymbol{\mu}_j^{(t)},
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_j^{(t)})
}.

Every row of responsibilities sums to one. A token with (0.8,0.2) contributes mostly to component 1 in the next update but still contributes to component 2.
These responsibilities provide the conditional probabilities needed to average over the unobserved component labels. This average is the expectation in the name of the algorithm. It replaces each unknown hard label with a probability vector under the current fit.

The maximization step updates the weights
The effective number of observations assigned to component k is

N_k^{(t)}
=
\sum_{i=1}^{N}
r_{ik}^{(t)}.

The updated mixing weight is

\pi_k^{(t+1)}
=
\frac{N_k^{(t)}}{N}.

The weights still sum to one because

\sum_{k=1}^{K}
N_k^{(t)}
=
\sum_{i=1}^{N}
\sum_{k=1}^{K}
r_{ik}^{(t)}
=
N.

Thus, dividing each effective count by N gives a valid set of mixture proportions.

Update the covariance
For an unrestricted Gaussian component, the covariance update is

\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k^{(t+1)}
=
\frac{1}{N_k^{(t)}}
\sum_{i=1}^{N}
r_{ik}^{(t)}
\left(
\mathbf{x}_i-
\boldsymbol{\mu}_k^{(t+1)}
\right)
\left(
\mathbf{x}_i-
\boldsymbol{\mu}_k^{(t+1)}
\right)^{\mathsf T}.

This is a responsibility-weighted average of outer products around the updated mean. Covariance restrictions change this update. A shared covariance pools information across components, while a spherical covariance retains only a restricted scale.
The chosen update must match the Gaussian component geometry declared in the model.

Introduce the unobserved label
Let

Z_i\in\{1,\ldots,K\}

be the unobserved component label for observation i. Before observing \mathbf{x}_i, the mixture gives

P(Z_i=k)=\pi_k.

Conditional on component k, the observation density is

p(\mathbf{x}_i\mid Z_i=k)
=
\mathcal{N}
(\mathbf{x}_i;
\boldsymbol{\mu}_k,
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k).

Bayes’ rule gives

P(Z_i=k\mid\mathbf{x}_i)
=
\frac{
P(Z_i=k)
p(\mathbf{x}_i\mid Z_i=k)
}{
p(\mathbf{x}_i)
}.

Substituting the mixture terms yields

P(Z_i=k\mid\mathbf{x}_i)
=
\frac{
\pi_k
\mathcal{N}
(\mathbf{x}_i;
\boldsymbol{\mu}_k,
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k)
}{
\sum_{j=1}^{K}
\pi_j
\mathcal{N}
(\mathbf{x}_i;
\boldsymbol{\mu}_j,
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_j)
}.

We write this probability as

r_{ik}
=
P(Z_i=k\mid\mathbf{x}_i).

The quantity r_{ik} is the responsibility of component k for observation i.

Why the probabilities sum to one
The denominator is the sum of all component contributions. Thus,

\sum_{k=1}^{K}r_{ik}
=
\frac{
\sum_{k=1}^{K}
\pi_k
\mathcal{N}
(\mathbf{x}_i;
\boldsymbol{\mu}_k,
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k)
}{
\sum_{j=1}^{K}
\pi_j
\mathcal{N}
(\mathbf{x}_i;
\boldsymbol{\mu}_j,
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_j)
}
=
1.

Membership is thus a normalization of the weighted densities. It does not require a separate distance rule.

State how cells are weighted
The validation loss should give the intended cells the intended weight. If one kind of cell is much more common than another, an unweighted mean may reward a representation that performs well only on the common kind.
Suppose binary cells are separated into present cells \mathcal{V}_1 and absent cells \mathcal{V}_0. A balanced squared loss is

\widehat{R}^{\mathrm{bal}}_{\mathcal{V}}(K)
=
\frac{1}{2}
\left[
\frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}_1|}
\sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{V}_1}
\left(1-\widehat{x}_{ij,K}\right)^2
+
\frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}_0|}
\sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{V}_0}
\widehat{x}_{ij,K}^2
\right].

This score gives presence and absence equal total weight. It estimates a different population average from a loss averaged over all cells in their observed proportions. The choice should be declared before the rank curve is inspected.

Construct one outer product
Let

\mathbf{a}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\2\\3
\end{bmatrix}

record the relative rate of three speakers, and let

\mathbf{b}^{\mathsf T}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
1&2&3
\end{bmatrix}

record the relative contribution of three rate measurements. Their outer product is

\mathbf{a}\mathbf{b}^{\mathsf T}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
a_1b_1&a_1b_2&a_1b_3\\
a_2b_1&a_2b_2&a_2b_3\\
a_3b_1&a_3b_2&a_3b_3
\end{bmatrix}.

Substituting the vector entries gives

\mathbf{X}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
1(1)&1(2)&1(3)\\
2(1)&2(2)&2(3)\\
3(1)&3(2)&3(3)
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
1&2&3\\
2&4&6\\
3&6&9
\end{bmatrix}.

Every cell obeys the same rule:

x_{ij}=a_i b_j.

The entry x_{23}=6 is not a separate parameter. It follows from a_2=2 and b_3=3.

Work one score by hand
Suppose a centered vowel token has two acoustic values

\mathbf{x}_i^{\mathsf T}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
2&1
\end{bmatrix},

and the first component direction is

\mathbf{v}_1
=
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\1
\end{bmatrix}.

The token’s first score is

\begin{aligned}
z_{i1}
&=
\begin{bmatrix}
2&1
\end{bmatrix}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\1
\end{bmatrix}\\
&=
\frac{2+1}{\sqrt{2}}\\
&=
\frac{3}{\sqrt{2}}\\
&\approx2.12.
\end{aligned}

The positive score means that this token lies in the positive direction defined by the two positive weights. The value 2.12 is not an original acoustic measurement. It is a coordinate in the component system.
If another token has centered row [-2,-1], its score is approximately -2.12. The two tokens lie on opposite sides of the mean along the same common direction.

Define one update
Let \mathbf{Y} be the incomplete data matrix, and let M be the set of its missing cells. Begin with a completed matrix \mathbf{X}^{(0)} by filling each missing cell with an initial value, often the observed mean of its column.
Let \mathcal{P}_K(\mathbf{X}^{(t)}) be the centered rank K reconstruction of the current completed matrix. One imputation update is

X_{ij}^{(t+1)}=
\begin{cases}
Y_{ij} & \text{if }(i,j)\notin M,\\
\left[\mathcal{P}_K(\mathbf{X}^{(t)})\right]_{ij}
& \text{if }(i,j)\in M.
\end{cases}

Observed values return to their measured values after every reconstruction. Only cells in M receive reconstructed values. The procedure stops when the missing cell updates change by less than a stated tolerance or when a maximum number of iterations is reached.

Work through two annotation records
Suppose the predicted affirmative gate probability is .70. For an affirmative gate with follow up category 6, suppose the conditional probability of category 6 is $.25. The joint contribution is

(.70)(.25)=.175.

For a negative gate, the contribution is

1-.70=.30.

No follow up factor appears in the second calculation. Substituting a numerical zero for the blank would instead assert that the annotator gave the lowest follow up response. The protocol did not produce that event.
The two cases can be calculated with a small function.


Code
gated_log_probability &lt;- function(gate, gate_probability, follow_probability = NA) {
  if (gate == 1) {
    log(gate_probability) + log(follow_probability)
  } else {
    log1p(-gate_probability)
  }
}

affirmative &lt;- gated_log_probability(
  gate = 1,
  gate_probability = 0.70,
  follow_probability = 0.25
)

negative &lt;- gated_log_probability(
  gate = 0,
  gate_probability = 0.70
)

exp(c(affirmative = affirmative, negative = negative))


The function requires a follow up probability only after an affirmative gate. This mirrors the instrument rather than filling a rectangular response column before the model is defined.
